
============================================================================
1. IS THE MISS A CONSTANT, OR IS IT TIMED?
============================================================================
  mean of loss / VaR90            -0.063

  autocorrelation of |loss / VaR90|  (scale-free, so the
  normality caveat above does not apply):
    lag 1  +0.046    lag 5  +0.047    lag 20  +0.025
  If the model had captured the conditional scale these would be ~0.
  Positive values mean volatility the model did not see coming.

============================================================================
2. HOW MUCH SCALE IS MISSING, MEASURED HONESTLY
============================================================================
  Same-day exposures run through a trailing realised covariance of
  the market moves, against the model's own scale on the same day.
  Both sides are conditional, so this comparison is valid where the
  two rejected in the note above are not.

    trailing covariance window    60d:   realised / model = 1.105
    trailing covariance window   250d:   realised / model = 1.096
    trailing covariance window  1000d:   realised / model = 1.081

  Modestly but persistently too narrow. Each market's own
  volatility is already filtered by its own GARCH, so what is
  missing is a PORTFOLIO-level quantity: the co-movement between
  markets, which a trend book is concentrated in by construction.

  worst 120-day stretch of loss/VaR90 ends 2004-05-10
  five largest standardised losses:
    2003-03-13  6.83 sigma
    2015-01-16  6.74 sigma
    2018-02-05  6.42 sigma
    2022-03-09  5.87 sigma
    2022-11-10  5.61 sigma

